---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import {
  renderCascade,
  renderDisproportion,
  renderRemandMix,
  renderForceChoropleth,
  renderRemandDuration,
} from '../lib/charts.mjs';

const cascade = renderCascade();
const disproportion = renderDisproportion();
const remandMix = renderRemandMix();
const choropleth = renderForceChoropleth();
const duration = renderRemandDuration();
---

<Base title="National picture — PRISM-R">
  <article>
    <h1 class="page-title">
      National picture
    </h1>

    <section class="mt-6 space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        This page traces youth remand disproportionality in England and Wales
        through the decisions that lead to it. It works at England and Wales
        level. The four charts are PRISM-R's own analysis of open data; they
        are distinct from the published headline statistics on the home page,
        and each is captioned with its source.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- CHART A: cascade -->
    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
        The road to remand
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-3 space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The Relative Rate Index compares the rate at which an ethnic group
          experiences a decision with the rate for White children. A value of
          one is parity; above one, the group is more likely to experience the
          decision. The cascade follows children through four decision points,
          from the first police contact to the court.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="cascade-chart mt-6" set:html={cascade} />
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
      <h3 class="font-sans text-base font-semibold text-ink">
        The groups do not follow one pattern
      </h3>
      <p>
        The four ethnic groups diverge across the cascade. For Black children
        the disparity is widest at the first point of contact: a stop and
        search rate 2.40 times the White rate. It is lower at each court stage,
        1.92 at remand and 1.59 at pooled custodial sentencing. For Asian
        children the pattern runs the other way, rising from 0.60 at stop and
        search and 0.52 at arrest, both below parity, to 1.51 at remand and
        1.38 at custodial sentencing.
      </p>
      <p>
        These are distinct findings. PRISM-R reports them separately rather
        than as a single account of disproportionality.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
      <h3 class="font-sans text-base font-semibold text-ink">
        Disparity appears before the court
      </h3>
      <p>
        Measurable disparity is present at stop and search and at arrest, for
        Black children and children of Mixed Heritage, before any court
        involvement. The
        disproportionality visible at remand is therefore not created at the
        court stage alone: a substantial part of it is already present in the
        policing decisions upstream. On these figures, much of the measured
        racial disparity is generated before a child reaches the court.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- CHART B: headline disproportion -->
    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
        Child population and custodial remand
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-3 space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Black children are about 6% of the 10 to 17 population of England and
          Wales, and about 26% of children remanded to youth detention
          accommodation in the year ending March 2025. The bars below set each
          ethnic group's share of the child population against its share of
          custodial remand.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="chart mt-6" set:html={disproportion} />
      <p class="caption font-sans">
        PRISM-R analysis. Child population: ONS Census 2021. Custodial remand,
        remand to youth detention accommodation: YJB Youth Justice Statistics
        2024-25, year ending March 2025. Retrieved May 2026.
        <a href="/data/manifest.json">See the build manifest</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- CHART C: remand decision mix -->
    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
        How remand decisions divide
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-3 space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Not every child subject to a remand decision is held. Most are
          bailed; the rest are placed on community remand, remanded to local
          authority accommodation, or remanded to custody. The bars below show
          how remand decisions divided for each ethnic group in the year ending
          March 2025. The custodial remand share, the darkest segment, is
          larger for Black children and children of Mixed Heritage than for
          White children.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="chart mt-6" set:html={remandMix} />
      <p class="caption font-sans">
        PRISM-R analysis of YJB Youth Justice Statistics 2024-25, year ending
        March 2025. Children in the Other category, and those of unknown
        ethnicity, are not shown: one cell for the Other category is suppressed
        under disclosure control, and unknown ethnicity is not a defined group.
        Retrieved May 2026.
        <a href="/data/manifest.json">See the build manifest</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed">
        The pattern is corroborated independently. HM Chief Inspector of
        Prisons' thematic review
        <a
          href="https://hmiprisons.justiceinspectorates.gov.uk/"
          class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2"
          >Children on short-term remand</a
        >
        (May 2026) cites the same figure, that 62% of children remanded to
        youth detention accommodation in the year ending March 2025 did not
        receive a custodial sentence, and reports that two in five children
        held in custody are now on remand, with nearly 100 children each year
        remanded only to be bailed or moved to local authority accommodation
        within two weeks.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- CHART D: stop-and-search choropleth by force -->
    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
        Where stop-and-search disparity is highest
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-3 space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The cascade's first stage varies sharply by place. Across the
          {choropleth.forces} police force areas shown, the stop-and-search
          Relative Rate Index for Black children runs from {choropleth.min} to
          {choropleth.max}, with a median of {choropleth.median}. The highest
          values are in forces with small Black child populations, where a
          modest number of searches produces a high rate against a small
          denominator; those values are real but less stable than the
          large-force figures. In {choropleth.suppressed} forces the Black
          search count falls below the disclosure threshold, so no value is
          shown.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="chart chart-map mt-6" set:html={choropleth.svg} />
      <p class="caption font-sans">
        Geographic variation in stop-and-search disparity. Each police force
        area is shaded by the RRI for Black children stopped and searched,
        year ending March 2025: the Black rate per 1,000 children divided by
        the White rate, both against the Census 2021 child population. Full
        interactive geographic exploration follows in the geographic explorer.
        PRISM-R analysis of Home Office Police powers and procedures open data;
        boundaries: ONS Open Geography portal. Retrieved May 2026.
        <a href="/data/manifest.json">See the build manifest</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- CHART E: remand duration by ethnicity -->
    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="font-sans text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
        How long children spend on remand
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-3 space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The remand population is the product of how many children enter
          remand and how long each remand lasts. A 25% reduction in the stock
          can therefore be achieved by remanding fewer children, by shortening
          remands, or both; and a reduction achieved by shortening is assessed
          for whether the shortening is equal across ethnic groups. The
          published episode data shows it currently is not. In every year
          since 2019, remand episodes for children from ethnic minority
          groups have lasted substantially longer at the median than those
          for White children: 74 nights against 40.5 in the year ending March
          2025, and 70 against 48 in the provisional 2025-26 year.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="chart mt-6" set:html={duration} />
      <p class="caption font-sans">
        Median nights per remand episode ending in each year:
        <span style="color:#1B3A5F; font-weight:600;">ethnic minority
        groups</span> against <span style="color:#5F5E5A;">White including
        white minorities</span>. PRISM-R analysis of YCS monthly youth custody
        report table 3.4, June 2026 edition. Four caveats: ethnicity is the
        binary published split, not the YJB five groups; the measure counts
        episodes ending in the year, not children on remand; no adjustment is
        made for offence mix or court tier; the 2025-26 year (marked *) is
        provisional. Retrieved September 2026.
        <a href="/data/manifest.json">See the build manifest</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

  <style>
    .chart :global(svg),
    .chart :global(figure) {
      max-width: 100%;
      height: auto;
    }
    .chart :global(figure) {
      margin: 0;
    }
    .caption {
      margin-top: 0.6rem;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: #6a6a6a;
    }
    .caption a {
      color: #1f3a5f;
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 2px;
    }

    /* Cascade chart, the hand-built inline SVG and its chrome. */
    .cascade-chart :global(figure.cascade) {
      margin: 0;
    }
    .cascade-chart :global(svg) {
      max-width: 100%;
      height: auto;
      display: block;
      margin-top: 0.4rem;
    }
    .cascade-chart :global(.cascade-title) {
      font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;
      font-size: 1rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      color: #1a1a1a;
      margin: 0;
    }
    .cascade-chart :global(.cascade-subtitle) {
      font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;
      font-size: 0.82rem;
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: #6a6a6a;
      margin: 0.35rem 0 0;
    }
    .cascade-chart :global(.cascade-legend) {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 0.4rem 1.1rem;
      list-style: none;
      padding: 0;
      margin: 0.8rem 0 0;
      font-family: Inter, system-ui, sans-serif;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      color: #444441;
    }
    .cascade-chart :global(.cascade-legend li) {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.4rem;
    }
    .cascade-chart :global(.sw) {
      width: 0.85rem;
      height: 0.85rem;
      border-radius: 2px;
      display: inline-block;
      flex: none;
    }
    .cascade-chart :global(.sw-baseline) {
      height: 0;
      border-radius: 0;
      border-top: 2px dashed #888780;
    }
    .cascade-chart :global(.cascade-finding) {
      font-size: 0.92rem;
      line-height: 1.6;
      color: #1a1a1a;
      margin: 0.9rem 0 0;
    }
    .cascade-chart :global(.caption) {
      margin-top: 0.6rem;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: #6a6a6a;
    }
    .cascade-chart :global(.caption a) {
      color: #1f3a5f;
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 2px;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
